# Rules for `specs`

- Include {{minSpecRows}}-{{maxSpecRows}} rows drawn from the known specs above.
- Useful labels for this category: {{specLabels}}.
- Copy spec values verbatim from the data above. Never invent numbers, prices or dates.
- Set `winner` on every row. Use `"tie"` only when the two are genuinely equivalent — no more
  than {{maxTiePercent}}% of rows may be ties.
- `verdict.winner` must be consistent with the table: do not declare A the overall winner when
  B wins more rows.

# Rules for `content`

Write GitHub-flavored markdown with at least {{minH2Sections}} `##` sections, in this order:

1. `## Overview` — two short paragraphs on why these two get cross-shopped.
2. `## {{entityA.name}} at a Glance` — one paragraph plus what stands out.
3. `## {{entityB.name}} at a Glance` — one paragraph plus what stands out.
4. `## Head-to-Head` — three or four `###` subsections covering the decision-driving
   differences for this category (performance, value, daily experience, longevity).
5. `## Who Should Buy Which` — explicit buyer profiles for each option.

Do not repeat the specs table, pros/cons lists, verdict or FAQ inside `content`. Those are
rendered separately from the other JSON fields.

# Tone and accuracy

- Second person, active voice, plain English. No marketing adjectives without evidence.
- All figures must come from the supplied data. If something is unknown, omit it.
- Never write "as an AI", "in today's fast-paced world", "when it comes to", or "in conclusion".
- No placeholder text of any kind.
- Be willing to name a clear winner. Use `"tie"` only when the two genuinely serve different buyers.
- Every FAQ question must be one a real shopper would type into Google.
